.\" ABSD manual page source (mdoc(7)); rendered by scripts/render-man.sh.
.Dd September 27, 2026
.Dt FILESYSTEM 7
.Os ABSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm filesystem
.Nd the ABSD filesystem and its file server
.Sh DESCRIPTION
ABSD has one filesystem, on the second half of its one disk.
The kernel knows nothing of files: it gives out a block device handle.
In the qualified boot exactly one process, the file server
.Nm absd-fsd ,
holds the only read-write handle to the disk, and every other process
reaches files through it, over a
.Em channel :
a pair of kernel pipes to the server.
Nothing else links the filesystem code over a writable disk; two writers
would corrupt it.
.Ss Channels are the authority
The server keeps, for each channel, a mount list (subtrees, each read-only
or read-write) and a table of the files opened through it.
A channel is therefore a capability: holding it is what lets a process see
files, and it sees exactly what the mount list allows.
.Pp
Paths are resolved by the server, lexically, under the channel's mount
list:
.Ql ..
cannot leave it, anything outside it does not exist for the client
.Pq Dq ABSD path not found ,
a read-only mount refuses every change
.Pq Dq ABSD filesystem is mounted read-only ,
and the directories leading to a mount point list only the way down.
There are no symbolic links, so resolution is exact.
.Pp
The SSH server binds each session's channel to:
.Bl -column "/tmp/session-N" "read-write" -offset indent
.It Pa /home/operator Ta read-write
.It Pa /tmp/session-N Ta read-write, this session only
.It Pa /bin Ta read-only
.It Pa /etc Ta read-only
.It Pa /share Ta read-only
.It Pa /var/log Ta read-only
.El
.Pp
No session's channel may include
.Pa /ssh ;
only the SSH server's own control channel reads it.
That channel sees
.Pa /ssh ,
.Pa /bin ,
and
.Pa /etc
read-only and
.Pa /var/log
read-write, where the SSH server writes its log and the fixed
.Pa network-status
sample; see
.Xr net-status 1 .
The file server writes
its own log into the filesystem directly.
A process can ask the server to bind a spare channel for a child with its
own mounts or fewer, never more; see
.Xr handles 7 .
When a session ends its channel is released: its open files are closed
and committed, and whatever the session left running is refused from then
on.
A release discards nothing the session sent while it was bound: a request
already waiting is read and refused, and bytes that break framing are
reported, before the channel goes quiet.
The channel is not bound again until that is done.
Binding the channel to a new session discards whatever predates the new
binding, so no request of an earlier session runs under a later one.
.Ss Objects
Files and directories, nothing else.
There are no owners, groups, permission bits, symbolic or hard links,
device nodes, or sparse files.
An object records one time, its modification time: the last change to a
file's content or size, or to a directory's entries, in whole seconds UTC
from the machine's clock when the file server made the change.
It is unknown (0) when no clock reading was available, and for files the
image builder installed.
There is no access, change, or creation time.
An object has one flag, read-only (no command sets it yet), and an identity:
the filesystem's random id and an object serial that is never reused.
The
.Nm stat
command prints them.
.Ss One namespace for every session
The server runs each request to completion before the next, from whatever
channel, so every session sees the same files at every moment.
Writes land in arrival order, each one whole; where two cover the same
bytes the later wins.
Creating a file that must not exist has exactly one winner.
A rename replaces its target atomically.
Locks are advisory, per open file, shared by every channel, and never wait:
a contended lock is refused at once.
A sequence of requests (look, then create) is not atomic against another
session's.
.Ss Commits
A
.Em commit
writes the whole metadata image to the half of the metadata area not in
use, flushes the disk, writes the superblock that selects it, and flushes
again.
That superblock write is the moment the change becomes durable.
.Pp
Creating, removing, and renaming (files and directories) are committed
before the request returns.
File data is committed when the file is closed, when
.Nm sync
is run, when the session ends, and at most about 200 ms after a write that
was not otherwise committed.
.Nm sync
returns once everything the server has accepted, from every session, is
committed and flushed;
.Ql sync -v
prints the commit sequence then on the disk, and
.Nm df
shows it too.
.Ss What survives a crash
Two failure models are qualified.
First, the machine stops abruptly (QEMU killed, or the kernel's stop) and
every sector the disk acknowledged is kept.
Second, a device law for power loss: any write not covered by a completed
flush may be lost or reordered, per aligned 512-byte or 4 KiB unit.
The second was enforced by a model of the device on the host and in the
guest; no real power was cut and no real drive's flush was tested.
.Pp
Under either model, after a crash the namespace is the one of the last
commit: a rename is never half done, and a committed file is whole.
Data written but not yet committed may be partly present or absent.
File contents carry no checksum: corruption of file data on the disk is
not detected.
.Ss Refusal and recovery
Mounting checks both superblocks, the digest of the selected metadata image,
and every structural rule of the namespace.
Anything wrong refuses the mount.
The mount never repairs and never falls back to the older superblock.
A disk that is entirely zero is formatted at first boot; so is a filesystem
whose format was interrupted before its first commit.
Any other disk without an ABSD layout is refused.
A filesystem of another format version (an image from before format 2)
is refused by name,
.Ql FSD FAILED mount: UnsupportedFormat ... ,
and never formatted over; it is moved to format 2 on the host by building
a new image from its files.
.Pp
In the qualified boot a refused mount, or a disk error while committing,
ends the file server
.Po
.Ql FSD FAILED ...
on the serial console
.Pc ,
and with it the boot.
There is no on-target repair tool, no
.Nm fsck ,
and no rescue boot.
The platform's host-side reader
.Pq Pa scripts/absd_fs.py
reads an image the mount would accept; it is a test tool, not a recovery
procedure.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr absd-sh 1 ,
.Xr handles 7 ,
.Xr process 7 ,
.Xr afterboot 8
.Pp
In the platform repository:
.Pa docs/FILESYSTEM.md
(format 2 and its operation contract) and
.Pa docs/POWER-LOSS.md
(the device law).
.Pp
A refused filesystem cannot be repaired on ABSD or on the host; the only
procedure is to build a new image
.Pq Xr afterboot 8 ,
copying files out first, while the old one still boots, with
.Ql ssh host cat FILE .
.Sh CAVEATS
The format is experimental, with no compatibility promise: a format change
means a new filesystem.
Every commit writes the whole metadata image, so its cost grows with the
number of objects, and the number of objects is bounded by a 64 KiB record
budget
.Pq Dq storage full .
.Nm df
counts data blocks only.
There are no quotas: every session shares the space.
